Q: Why does @Transactional sometimes not work?

Main reason: Spring applies transactions with **proxy-based AOP**.
The transactional advice runs only when the call goes **through the Spring proxy**.

────────────────────────────────────────
1️⃣ How it works

@Transactional
public void createUser() { ... }

Client → Proxy (begin) → real method → Proxy (commit/rollback)

────────────────────────────────────────
2️⃣ Classic failure: self-invocation (internal call)

@Service
public class UserService {

    public void methodA() {
        methodB(); // ❌ this.methodB() — bypasses proxy
    }

    @Transactional
    public void methodB() { ... }
}

userService.methodA() from outside:
Client → Proxy → methodA → direct methodB (no proxy) → @Transactional ignored

Fix options:
1) Move @Transactional method to another Spring bean (best/clear)
2) Self-inject the proxy and call through it (advanced; avoid unless needed)
3) AspectJ weave mode (rare setup)

────────────────────────────────────────
3️⃣ Other common reasons it “doesn’t work”

| Issue                         | Why                                      |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| private / package-private*    | Proxy can’t advise properly (*public required for Spring proxy) |
| final method / final class    | CGLIB can’t override                     |
| Calling from constructor      | Bean/proxy not ready                     |
| Not a Spring bean             | `new UserService()` → no proxy           |
| Checked exception             | Default rollback is RuntimeException/Error only; use `rollbackFor = Exception.class` |
| Wrong proxy type expectations | Same-class call still bypasses           |
| DB engine without txn         | e.g. non-InnoDB quirks                   |

Default rollback rule:
- RuntimeException / Error → rollback
- checked Exception → commit unless rollbackFor set

────────────────────────────────────────
4️⃣ Propagation (quick)

| Propagation   | Behavior                                      |
| ------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| REQUIRED      | Join existing or create new (**default**)     |
| REQUIRES_NEW  | Always new txn; suspend current               |
| SUPPORTS      | Join if present, else non-tx                  |
| MANDATORY     | Must already have txn, else exception         |
| NOT_SUPPORTED | Suspend txn; run non-tx                       |
| NEVER         | Fail if txn exists                            |
| NESTED        | Nested txn via JDBC savepoints (if supported) |

Most used in interviews: **REQUIRED**, **REQUIRES_NEW**.
Remember: propagation also only applies when called **through a proxy**.

Example interview contrast:
- REQUIRED → same txn; child fail can roll back parent
- REQUIRES_NEW → separate txn; child fail doesn’t automatically undo parent

────────────────────────────────────────
5️⃣ Best interview answer

“@Transactional is proxy-based. If a method calls another @Transactional method in the same class, the call doesn’t go through the proxy,
so no transaction starts. It also won’t work on non-public methods, non-Spring beans, or when exceptions don’t match rollback rules.
Fix by putting transactional methods on another bean (or calling via proxy) and using public methods.”

